.TH SIGSETOPS 3P "August 1, 1992"
.ds ]W POSIX 1003.1
.SH NAME
sigemptyset, sigfillset, sigaddset, sigdelset, sigismember - (POSIX only) manipulate signal sets.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <signal.h>
.sp
.B int sigemptyset(sigset_t *\fIset\fP);
.PP
.B int sigfillset(sigset_t *\fIset\fP);
.PP
.B int sigaddset(sigset_t *\fIset\fP, int \fIsigno\fP);
.PP
.B int sigdelset(sigset_t *\fIset\fP, int \fIsigno\fP);
.PP
.B int sigismember(const sigset_t *\fIset\fP, int \fIsigno\fP);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The information in this specification applies
only to POSIX applications.
.PP
The \fIsigsetops\fR primitives manipulate sets of signals.  They operate on
data objects addressable by the application, not on any set of
signals known to the system, such as the set blocked from delivery
to a process or the set pending for a process.
.PP
The \fIsigemptyset\fR function initializes the signal set pointed to by the
argument \fIset\fR, such that all signals are excluded.
.PP
The \fIsigfillset\fR function initializes the signal set pointed to by the
argument \fIset\fR, such that all signals are included.
.PP
The \fIsigaddset\fR and \fIsigdelset\fR functions respectively add or
delete the individual signal specified by the value of the argument \fIsigno\fR
to or from the signal set pointed to by the argument \fIset\fR.
.PP
The \fIsigismember\fR function tests whether the signal specified
by the value of the argument \fIsigno\fR is a member of the set
pointed to by the argument \fIset\fR.
.SH RETURNS
Upon successful completion, the \fIsigismember\fR function returns
a value of one if the specified signal is a member of the specified
set, or a value of zero if it is not.  Upon successful completion,
the other functions return a value of zero.  For all of the above
functions, if an error is detected, a value of -1 is returned, and
\fIerrno\fR is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP 15
[EINVAL]
The value of the \fIsigno\fR argument is an invalid or unsupported signal
number.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sigaction(2P),
sigprocmask(2P)
